1
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:33,000
Scooby Goes Hollywood is a animation, comedy, tv
movie film directed by Ray Patterson.

2
00:04:54,000 --> 00:05:00,000
Shaggy and Scooby-Doo quit their Saturday morning TV
series in pursuit of Hollywood stardom.

3
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:27,000
Shaggy Rogers (voice), played by Casey Kasem.

4
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:54,000
Scooby-Doo (voice), played by Don Messick.

5
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:21,000
Fred Jones (voice), played by Frank Welker.

6
00:14:42,000 --> 00:14:48,000
Daphne Blake (voice), played by Heather North.

7
00:17:09,000 --> 00:17:15,000
Velma Dinkley (voice), played by Patricia Stevens.

8
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:42,000
Kerry / Girl Fan / Executive Secretary (voice), played by Ginny McSwain.

9
00:22:03,000 --> 00:22:09,000
Scooby Goes Hollywood (later released on home video as Scooby-Doo Goes Hollywood) is a 1979 animated
television special starring the cast of Hanna-Barbera's Saturday-morning cartoon series Scooby-Doo.

10
00:24:30,000 --> 00:24:36,000
It was originally broadcast on ABC on December 23, 1979 to
celebrate the 10th anniversary of the franchise.

11
00:26:57,000 --> 00:27:03,000
A musical-based parody of both the Scooby-Doo formula and of Hollywood in general, the story line centered on Shaggy convincing Scooby that both of them deserve better than being stars in what he considers a low-class Saturday morning show, and attempts to pitch a number of potential prime-time
shows to network executive "C.J." (voiced by Rip Taylor), all of which are parodies of movies and then-popular TV shows which are How The West Was Won, Happy Days, Laverne & Shirley, Superman, The Sound of Music, Saturday Night Fever, Sonny & Cher, The Love Boat, and Charlie's Angels.

12
00:29:24,000 --> 00:29:30,000
Caught in the middle of this ordeal are Fred, Daphne, Velma, and Scooby's loyal Saturday morning fan
base; all of whom convince Scooby to come back to his Saturday morning television show.

13
00:31:51,000 --> 00:31:57,000
The special was first released on VHS by Worldvision Enterprises in the
1980s, and again later by Warner Home Video in 1997.

14
00:34:18,000 --> 00:34:24,000
The special was released on DVD by Warner Bros.

15
00:36:45,000 --> 00:36:51,000
on June 4, 2002.

16
00:39:12,000 --> 00:39:18,000
The special was included as a bonus feature in
the Blu-ray release of Scooby-Doo!

17
00:41:39,000 --> 00:41:45,000
and the Reluctant Werewolf in 2024.

18
00:44:06,000 --> 00:44:12,000
The film explores themes of california, talking dog, hollywood.

19
00:46:33,000 --> 00:46:39,000
The tone is lighthearted.
